  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "How many times must a coin be tossed to be at least 95% certain that you will obtain at least one head?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "5 tosses.\n\nUse the complement: the probability of all tails must be at most $0.05$.",
      "worked": "Let $A$ be the event of obtaining at least one head in $n$ tosses. Then $A'$ is the event of obtaining tails on every toss.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(A)&=1-\\left(\\frac12\\right)^n\\\\\\Pr(A)&\\ge\\frac{19}{20}\\end{align*}$$\nThus, $\\left(\\frac12\\right)^n\\le\\frac1{20}$.\n\nFor four tosses, $\\left(\\frac12\\right)^4=\\frac1{16}>\\frac1{20}$, so four tosses are insufficient.\n\nFor five tosses, $\\left(\\frac12\\right)^5=\\frac1{32}<\\frac1{20}$, so five tosses are sufficient. Since the probability of all tails decreases with each additional toss, the minimum is $5$ tosses.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
